ys  um united states and in the west would  be blamed by this by russian public  opinion  all right that was dw russians vladimir  yazipuff joining us from london thank  you so much for that insight  and i'm joined now from brussels by our  nato correspondent terry schultz uh  terry germany also now withdrawing  embassy staff and advising citizens to  leave ukraine following you know as we  just heard similar action by the united  states  um the biden administration seems to  think we're at a point of outright  conflict  are they sharing the intelligence about  this with their nato allies  they do seem to think that outright  conflict is at least very possible more  possible than it has been up until now  and they are sharing that intelligence  you heard from richard that president  biden held a video call with key  european leaders and last night at nato  headquarters there was a meeting of all  30 nato ambassadors to share the  intelligence from the united states and  something was really striking after this  meeting and leading up into it nick and  that's that until now european countries  had really been resisting the u.s uh  move to pull it to pull their citizens  out of ukraine to advise them to leave  and once this intelligence just started  coming out yesterday you saw  many many european allies doing just  that starting with norway then the  netherlands and the uk  and now of course we've seen many more  including germany and that says to me  that this intelligence is very credible  that the u.s was able to share with  allies exactly what it was that was  leading them to believe that indeed an  invasion could be imminent  and we just saw of course uh terry the  nato secretary general in that report  welcoming american troops to romania so  nato's ratcheting up its military  posture in in the allied countries that  border ukraine how much of a concern is  that any russian military action could  spread beyond ukraine's borders  well one a key factor is that i